course: adult-life
lesson: 23
title: When Grandma and Grandad need more help
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: As people grow very old, their bodies and sometimes their minds get weaker, and they need more help with everyday things like walking, washing, meals and medicine.
explain: Some illnesses in old age never go away; doctors call them chronic, and they can be controlled but not cured.
explain: Picture a Saturday in Toronto: Mum grabs her keys again and says, "I'm popping round to Gran's to do her tablets and her tea." She goes every day, after work, before bed.
explain: Families have hard choices to make: a carer at home, a care home, or looking after Grandma themselves. They may feel guilty, tired or argue about it.
explain: When an old person gets confused or calls you the wrong name, it is the illness, not a lack of love.
fact: A chronic condition is an illness that lasts a long time or for life, like arthritis, asthma or heart disease; it can often be controlled but not cured.
fact: Diabetes means the body cannot control the sugar in the blood properly; a small machine measures blood sugar from a drop of blood from the finger.
fact: Diabetes is not caused by eating one too many sweets, and with care people with diabetes live normal lives.
fact: With age, muscles weaken and balance gets worse, so older people may wobble, shuffle, or use a stick or a frame.
fact: A fall is much more serious for an old person because their bones are thinner and break more easily; "kept her in" means she had to stay in hospital.
fact: A care home is a place where older people live with staff who help them wash, dress, eat and take medicine, day and night.
fact: A carer is someone paid to help a person with everyday things like getting up, washing and meals, so they can stay in their own home.
fact: Many grown-ups become unpaid carers for their own parents, helping with medicine and meals, which can leave them tired and stressed.
fact: Dementia is an illness of the brain, more common in old age, that slowly damages memory and thinking; it gets worse over time and there is no cure yet.
fact: A person with dementia may mix up names or think it is years ago; correcting them sharply can upset them.
fact: Forgetting a name now and then, "on the tip of my tongue", is completely normal at any age and is not dementia.
fact: "She's not herself" means someone is behaving differently than usual, from illness, worry, grief or medicine; grown-ups say it to be kind and private.
exercise: "Grandma's got a chronic condition. It doesn't go away." What does chronic mean here? | It lasts a long time or for life | It is very sudden and very dangerous | It is catching, so keep away from her | It will be cured by next week
exercise: Which of these is an example of a chronic condition? | Arthritis | A broken arm | A cut finger | A cold
exercise: Your uncle pricks his finger before lunch and checks a little machine. What is he most likely doing? | Measuring his blood sugar because he has diabetes | Checking whether his lunch has gone off before he eats it | Testing if his finger is still working | Taking his temperature to see if he has a cold
exercise: Your uncle with diabetes suddenly goes pale and confused. What should you do? | Fetch an adult straight away | Give him more of his insulin | Let him have a nap on the sofa | Wait to see if it passes by itself
exercise: "Grandad isn't as steady on his feet as he was." Why does this happen with age? | Muscles weaken and balance gets worse | His shoes have become far too big for his feet | Old people walk slowly on purpose | The floors in old houses are not flat
exercise: How can you help Grandad when he visits? | Keep toys off the floor and offer your arm on stairs | Race him up the stairs every time he comes, to keep him fit | Move the furniture so he gets more exercise | Take away his walking stick so he gets stronger
exercise: "Nan had a fall and they kept her in." What does "kept her in" mean? | She had to stay in hospital | She was not allowed out of her room at home | She was put in prison for falling | She stayed inside because it was raining
exercise: Why is a fall more serious for Nan than for you? | Her bones are thinner and break more easily | Old people fall from much higher up than children do | Children's bones are made of rubber | Nan falls on purpose to get attention
exercise: "We're looking at care homes for Great-Granny." What is a care home? | A place where old people live with staff who help them day and night | A hospital where old people go for an operation | A hotel where families go on holiday together | A school where old people go to learn to cook, read and swim all over again
exercise: Why do families choose a care home? | It is no longer safe for the person to live alone | They want to get rid of the old person | Care homes are always free and very fun for everyone who lives there | Old people must go to one at seventy
exercise: "A carer comes in twice a day to help Grandpa." What does the carer do? | Helps him with everyday things like getting up, washing and meals | Cleans the windows and mows the lawn for money | Visits to check he is paying his bills on time to the gas and electric | Is a relative who lives in his spare room
exercise: Mum grabs her keys: "I'm popping round to Gran's to do her tablets and her tea." What is Mum doing? | Caring for her own mother, without being paid | Going to a café in town to drink tea with her friends | Taking Gran's tablets for her own headache | Visiting Gran once a year for her birthday
exercise: Mum is tired from looking after Gran as well as you. What helps her most? | Saying thank you and helping at home | Asking her to drive you to the park | Telling her Gran should look after herself | Keeping out of the way and never asking
exercise: "Grandma has dementia." What is dementia? | An illness of the brain that slowly damages memory and thinking | Being grumpy because you are old and tired of everything around you | A sore throat that old people often catch | Forgetting your keys once in a while
exercise: Granny calls you by your mum's name. What is the kind thing to do? | Smile and say your name gently | Shout at her that she got it wrong | Laugh at her in front of everybody | Stop visiting her because she forgot you
exercise: Dad snaps his fingers: "What's his name again? It's on the tip of my tongue." Does Dad have dementia? | No, forgetting a name now and then is normal | Yes, forgetting any name at all means dementia | Yes, and he will soon forget everything | No, he is only pretending to forget
exercise: Mum lowers her voice on the phone: "She's not herself at the moment." What does she mean? | The person is behaving differently than usual | The person has changed her name to a new one by law | The person has a twin sister who is visiting | The person is on holiday in another country
test: Your sister says, "Uncle's got diabetes 'cause he ate too many sweets at Christmas." How do you put her right? | Diabetes is not caused by eating a few too many sweets | She is right, one big Christmas causes diabetes | Diabetes comes from not eating sweets at all | Diabetes is caught from other people at parties
test: Dad on the phone: "Nan's gone over again, broke her hip this time, they're keeping her in." What happened? | Nan fell, broke her hip and is staying in hospital | Nan went over to a friend's and stayed for tea | Nan broke a dish and is keeping it as a souvenir | Nan went on holiday and is staying longer than planned
test: Aunt and Mum argue: "I can't keep doing this every night!" "So you want to dump her in a home?" Why are they arguing? | Deciding about a care home is hard and families feel guilty | They both want to be the one to move into the home | They are arguing about whose house is bigger | Care homes are only for people nobody loves
test: Grandma asks you three times in an hour, "Have you had your tea, pet?" She has dementia. What is the best thing to do? | Answer kindly each time | Tell her crossly she already asked | Ignore her until she stops | Tell her she is being silly
test: Your brother says, "Gran called me Uncle Mike, she doesn't even love me anymore." How do you put him right? | Her illness mixes up names, she still loves him | He is right, mixed-up names mean no love | Gran was joking because she likes Uncle Mike more | Gran did it on purpose to make him angry
test: Mum, exhausted: "Work, then Gran's, then you lot, I'm knackered." What is happening? | Mum is caring for Gran on top of work and family | Mum has a second job working at Gran's café | Mum is cross because Gran has moved in | Mum is training for a long-distance race
test: A neighbour says, "The carer's in and out in ten minutes, it's a joke." What is likely true? | Carers often have many visits and are rushed | Carers are paid to visit for only a minute | The carer does not want to help at all | Carers only come to drop off the post
test: Dad laughs, "I walked into the kitchen and forgot why, I must be going senile!" Is he right? | No, forgetting little things now and then is normal | Yes, it is the first sign of dementia for sure | Yes, everyone over forty has dementia | No, he did it on purpose to make you laugh
test: Grandad, cross: "I don't need a bloody stick, I'm not an invalid!" Why might he say this? | He hates losing his independence | Sticks are only used by people with broken legs | He is planning to go running every morning | The stick is too heavy for him to lift
test: Mum whispers, "Mrs Green's not herself, keep an eye on her." What should you understand? | Mrs Green is acting differently and may be unwell or upset | Mrs Green has been replaced by a different woman | Mrs Green is in trouble with the police | Mrs Green wants you to watch her house for her
test: The news: "More people are living longer with chronic illnesses like heart disease." What does chronic mean? | Lasting a long time and often not curable | Very short and quickly cured | Only found in young children | Caught from other people on buses
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises dementia? | A progressive illness of the brain, impairing memory and thinking | The occasional forgetting of a name, experienced by persons of all ages | A deliberate refusal on the part of the elderly to remember | A temporary confusion invariably cured within a few days
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a fall constitutes a graver risk to an elderly person than to a child. | The bones of the elderly are thinner and fracture more readily | The elderly invariably fall from a considerably greater height | Children are immune from injury of any description | The elderly are not permitted to receive hospital treatment
exam: It is given that a relative "attends daily to the medication and meals" of an elderly parent without remuneration. Determine the most accurate description of that relative. | An unpaid carer | A care home manager | A medical practitioner | A hired domestic cleaner
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a chronic condition. | It persists over a long period and may be controlled but not cured | It arises suddenly and resolves entirely within a single week | It is transmitted readily from one person to another | It affects exclusively persons below the age of ten years
